{
 "lead": "A long, straight column under axial compression can suddenly bend sideways and fail, although the material has not crushed. This sideways failure is buckling, and the load at which it first becomes possible is Euler's crippling load. For a pin-ended column it is P = π²EI/L². The same expression covers fixed, free and mixed ends when L is replaced by an effective length K L. Designers use it for struts in roof trusses, crane jacks, scaffolding and chassis links, where a thin compression member must not buckle.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#columns-and-struts-classification-and-end-conditions\">7.1</a> for end conditions and radius of gyration, Unit 4 for the bending equation, and Unit 5 for the elastic curve of a bent beam.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "The pin-ended column and the sine curve",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Take a perfectly straight column of length L, pinned at both ends, and give it a small sideways deflection y at distance x from one end. The axial load P is vertical, so at that section its lever arm is y and the bending moment is M = P y. The moment acts to bend the column back toward the straight line, so the bending equation carries a minus sign."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "EI d²y/dx² = −P y",
     "where": "E is Young's modulus (N/m²); I is the least second moment of area (m⁴); y is lateral deflection (m); x is distance along the column (m); P is axial load (N)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Put α² = P/(EI). Then d²y/dx² + α² y = 0. The solution is y = A sin αx + B cos αx. At the lower pin x = 0 and y = 0, so B = 0. At the upper pin x = L and y = 0, so A sin αL = 0. A = 0 gives the straight column. A bent shape first becomes possible when sin αL = 0, and the smallest nonzero root is αL = π. Thus α² = π²/L²."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "P = π²EI/L²",
     "where": "P is Euler's crippling load (N); E is Young's modulus (N/m²); I is the least second moment of area (m⁴); L is the pin-ended length (m)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Higher roots αL = 2π, 3π give higher buckling loads and more waves. Real columns buckle at the lowest one."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Four end conditions and effective length",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Pinned ends cannot move sideways but can rotate freely. Fixed ends cannot rotate. A free end can move and rotate. Euler's differential equation is unchanged; the distance between points of zero bending moment changes. Replace L by the effective length Le = K L."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "P = π²EI/(K L)²",
     "where": "K is the effective length factor (dimensionless); K L = Le is the effective length (m); the other symbols are as above."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "End condition",
      "Effective length Le",
      "K",
      "Crippling load"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Both ends pinned",
       "L",
       "1",
       "π²EI/L²"
      ],
      [
       "Both ends fixed",
       "L/2",
       "0.5",
       "4π²EI/L²"
      ],
      [
       "One fixed, one pinned",
       "about 0.7L",
       "about 0.7",
       "about 2.04π²EI/L²"
      ],
      [
       "One fixed, one free",
       "2L",
       "2",
       "π²EI/(4L²)"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The buckled shapes differ. Both ends pinned gives one half-sine bow over L. Both ends fixed gives an S-shape made of two half-sine segments of length L/2, with inflection points at L/4 and 3L/4. One fixed and one pinned gives one half-sine segment of length about 0.7L. One fixed and one free gives a quarter-wave of length 2L. The fixed-fixed column carries four times the pinned-pinned load; the fixed-free column carries one quarter."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Assumptions and real joints",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Euler's result assumes a perfectly straight column, homogeneous material, load exactly along the axis, small deflection, and stress below the proportional limit. It also assumes ideal ends: frictionless pins or perfect fixity. Real columns have initial crookedness, load eccentricity and residual stress. Real bolted, welded or riveted joints rotate a little, so they sit between pinned and fixed. Use an effective length that matches the joint, not the ideal value blindly. The formula is unsafe for short, thick columns, which crush before they buckle; check slenderness limits in <a href=\"#effective-length-slenderness-ratio-and-limits-of-euler-s-theory\">7.3</a> and use <a href=\"#rankine-gordon-formula-for-columns\">7.4</a> for the full range. Always take I about the weak axis. A safe design uses a factor of safety on the crippling load and checks both buckling and yielding."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
